{
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "score": 2,
  "estimated_minutes": 10,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only to understand that Barnett left Aspect for a competing executive role and Aspect sought a preliminary injunction enforcing Section 7(a)(iv)."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The challenge to “reasonably likely” concerns familiar contract principles of indefiniteness and ambiguity, not technical knowledge of contact-center software."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The defect is confined to “reasonably likely” in Section 7(a)(iv), with no need to reconstruct obligations across other provisions or agreements."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Lines 44–45 plainly identify Barnett’s challenge and the court’s rejection of it for preliminary-injunction purposes, without assigning a numerical probability to “reasonably likely.”"
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The definiteness challenge to “reasonably likely” is readily distinguished from whether Barnett’s particular Avaya employment breached the covenant."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d01: The only subtlety is distinguishing Barnett’s alternative indefiniteness and ambiguity arguments from the court’s limited conclusion that Aspect was likely to establish enforceability; the court did not supply a precise probability threshold.",
  "reason": "A generalist reviewer can handle this case with a short, targeted read of Section 7(a)(iv), the employment transition, and lines 44–45. Barnett sought either nonenforcement for vagueness or resort to extrinsic evidence for ambiguity, while Aspect sought enforcement of the restriction. The court states its response directly, subject to the preliminary-injunction posture. The broader choice-of-law dispute, trade-secret evidence, and later motion to amend need not be mastered to recognize the listed defect."
}
